    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d327">

                <pb facs="0550" n="504" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg504"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5914/49½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-12-05">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">5 December, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I am glad the Danish
                    Minister gave you an opportunity to be so frank with him, and I hope he realizes
                    how entirely friendly to Denmark the frankness was. It would appear from the
                    enclosed message from the Danish Minister of Foreign Affairs<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d327-f1"/> that he does.</p>
                <p>I hope that you will do as you suggest, namely, first ascertain just what is
                    meant by the occupation of Greenland and, should that matter be satisfactorily
                    cleared up, proceed at once to the negotiation of a treaty for the purchase of
                    the Danish West Indies. The opportunity <pb facs="0551" n="505" xml:id="pg505"/>
                    has apparently come, and we may be able to relieve the Danish Government of a
                    considerable embarrassment.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
